.TH GXWI-MY-SETTINGS 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-my-settings \- change your own name, password, SSH keys and language on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-my-settings
.SH DESCRIPTION
My Settings is the window for a person's own account: the settings that
belong to them on the system rather than to a desktop. Open it from the
launcher (type my). It takes no arguments. Run with none and no desktop to
open on, it says so and points to
.BR passwd (1)
and
.BR lps (1),
which do the same at a terminal.
.PP
Everything in it acts on whoever is looking, and on nobody else. The
daemons it asks take the person from their token and are told no name, so
no one can change another's account here. It has four sections.
.PP
Account shows the account name, the display name and the sign-in method.
Display Name sets the name shown beside the account name, with Apply once
it is changed; it asks for no password, and the desktop shows it from the
next sign-in. The account name and the sign-in method are an
administrator's to change, in Principals Manager.
.PP
Password changes the password used to sign in here and over SSH. It asks
for the current password and the new one twice, and the authority checks
them by the same rules as anywhere else. The line along the bottom says
what the authority said, and the password fields are emptied whatever
happened. The new password is used from the next sign-in.
.PP
SSH Keys lists the person's keys by label, fingerprint, type and the day
each was added. Add Key opens a form for the one line of a .pub file and the
password; Remove asks for the password under the key before taking it away.
.PP
Every change to a password or key asks for the current password first: a
desktop left signed in is not proof that the person at it is its owner. An
account with no password to give, one that signs in with keys only, sees
why in place of these forms, and its keys are an administrator's to change.
.PP
Language & Formats chooses the person's language and how dates, numbers,
currency, measurements and paper sizes are written, from the languages
installed. System Default and Match Language remove the person's own value.
Each choice is written as it is made, under CurrentUser\eLocale, which is
the person's Users\e<SID>\eLocale, and applies from the next sign-in.
Whether it may be changed is asked of the registry; if someone has narrowed
who may, the section is shown read-only and says so.
.PP
The account is read again every five seconds, so a change made elsewhere
shows here.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-my-settings.toml
Its application declaration.
.TP
.I /run/lpsd/self.sock
lpsd's self socket, where the account is read and the display name set.
.TP
.I /run/logon.sock
The authority's logon socket, where the password and keys are changed.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-peios/locale
The installed languages it offers.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR passwd (1),
.BR lps (1).
.PP
The passwd command, The lps command (Changing your own keys) and Session
locales, at learn.peios.org.
